From 85638f8640cba6a3134259121dbf45e2b38afd25 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: mesa-nvk-horizon <noreply@invalid>
Date: Sun, 9 Aug 2026 16:16:40 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] vulkan/wsi: Horizon, only the window's owner may close the
 framebuffer

mesa-nvk-horizon: Phase 6 item 4 (Horizon WSI backend)
Why: libnx's framebufferClose calls nwindowReleaseBuffers(fb->win)
 unconditionally (nx/source/display/framebuffer.c), which disconnects
 the producer and frees every slot the BufferQueue believes it holds -
 whoever registered them. This backend called it from
 vkDestroySwapchainKHR outside the surface lock and with no ownership
 test at all, so destroying a superseded copy-fallback swapchain reached
 into a window a *different* swapchain owned by then and disconnected
 it. That is the ordinary recreation sequence - create the new
 swapchain, then destroy the old - and it is also legal concurrency:
 vkDestroySwapchainKHR externally synchronises only the swapchain it is
 given, so an application may run it beside another thread's acquire and
 present on a different one.
Evidence: hardware, both directions, same test and same tree with only
 this patch differing.
 .
 WITH IT, run 17 (2026-08-09, build 2026-08-09T14:35:22.614Z
 6f833f5-dirty mesa:597ea0a), t_vk_wsi_mt: RESULT PASS (50/50), which
 includes "A/copy: after the retired swapchain was destroyed the
 survivor presented 30 of 30 frames" and 40 further generations of the
 same sequence with the destroy on another thread.
 .
 WITHOUT IT, run 18 (build 2026-08-09T14:57:27.459Z 6f833f5-dirty
 mesa:3fe711d, the revert of this commit), the console took a system
 fatal. The log ends at
   ok A/copy: an acquire on the retired swapchain -> OUT_OF_DATE_KHR
 with no line after it; the next statement in the test is the
 vkDestroySwapchainKHR on that retired copy-fallback swapchain.
 atmosphere/fatal_reports/01786287644_0100000000001000.log records
 "Result: 0x290 (2144-0001)" in process qlaunch - the system's own home
 menu, not this process - on firmware 22.5.0 / Atmosphere 1.11.2. Both
 logs are in docs/hw-logs/.
Upstream: no - this file is the Horizon backend itself.

The framebuffer is now closed at the moment the swapchain stops owning
the window, which is one of exactly two places: wsi_horizon_claim_window
when a later swapchain evicts it, and wsi_horizon_swapchain_destroy when
it is still the owner. Both hold the surface lock, both test ownership,
and every framebuffer is still closed exactly once - an evicted
swapchain has none left to close.

The zero-copy path never had this: it registers through
nwindowConfigureBuffer and gives the window back through
wsi_horizon_release_window, which has tested ownership since 0053. Only
the copy fallback owns a libnx Framebuffer, and only its teardown
reached past the ownership test - which is why every recorded Phase 6
run missed it. t_vk_swapchain's section D runs this exact sequence on
the zero-copy path and passes.
---
 src/vulkan/wsi/wsi_horizon.c | 66 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-----
 1 file changed, 58 insertions(+), 8 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/vulkan/wsi/wsi_horizon.c b/src/vulkan/wsi/wsi_horizon.c
index 9bd603a..4073d16 100644
--- a/src/vulkan/wsi/wsi_horizon.c
+++ b/src/vulkan/wsi/wsi_horizon.c
@@ -846,6 +846,28 @@ wsi_horizon_fill_graphic_buffer(struct wsi_horizon_swapchain *chain,
 
 static void wsi_horizon_release_slots(struct wsi_horizon_swapchain *chain);
 
+/* Closes the copy fallback's framebuffer, if this swapchain has one.
+ *
+ * IT IS A WINDOW OPERATION, NOT A PRIVATE ONE, and that is the whole
+ * reason it has a name. libnx's framebufferClose calls
+ * nwindowReleaseBuffers(fb->win) unconditionally
+ * (nx/source/display/framebuffer.c), which disconnects the producer and
+ * frees every slot the BufferQueue believes it holds — whoever
+ * registered them. So it may only be called by the swapchain that
+ * currently owns the window's registration, and the two callers below
+ * are the two moments a swapchain stops owning it: eviction and
+ * teardown. Both hold the surface lock.
+ */
+static void
+wsi_horizon_close_framebuffer(struct wsi_horizon_swapchain *chain)
+{
+   if (!chain->fb_created)
+      return;
+
+   framebufferClose(&chain->fb);
+   chain->fb_created = false;
+}
+
 /* Takes the window's registration for `chain`, evicting whoever had it.
  * Called with the surface unlocked; takes the lock itself. */
 /* `old` is pCreateInfo->oldSwapchain resolved to this backend's type, or
@@ -894,6 +916,24 @@ wsi_horizon_claim_window(struct wsi_horizon_swapchain *chain,
        */
       prev->presentable = false;
 
+      /* ITS FRAMEBUFFER GOES NOW, WHILE IT IS STILL THE OWNER, and that
+       * is a fix rather than tidiness. framebufferClose disconnects the
+       * producer (see wsi_horizon_close_framebuffer), so leaving it for
+       * the evicted swapchain's vkDestroySwapchainKHR meant that call
+       * reached into a window this swapchain owns by then and
+       * disconnected it — after which every dequeue on the *new*
+       * swapchain answers NO_INIT, which the acquire loop reads as "the
+       * queue is full, come back later", so it waits for a buffer that
+       * can never arrive.
+       *
+       * That is the ordinary recreate sequence — create the new
+       * swapchain, then destroy the old — on the copy fallback, and it
+       * is also legal concurrency: vkDestroySwapchainKHR externally
+       * synchronises only the swapchain it is given, so it may run
+       * beside another thread's acquire and present on a different one.
+       */
+      wsi_horizon_close_framebuffer(prev);
+
       Result rc = nwindowReleaseBuffers(prev->window);
       if (R_FAILED(rc)) {
          mesa_logw("wsi_horizon: nwindowReleaseBuffers on the previous "
@@ -1732,15 +1772,23 @@ wsi_horizon_swapchain_destroy(struct wsi_swapchain *wsi_chain,
     * bqCancelBuffer is IPC and this holds a mutex across it, which is
     * worth doing here: the lock is per surface and only contended by
     * swapchain creation and destruction, never per frame. */
+   /* The framebuffer is closed here too, and under the same test, for
+    * the reason wsi_horizon_close_framebuffer gives: closing it is a
+    * disconnect of whatever producer the window currently has. It used
+    * to happen below, outside the lock and with no ownership test at
+    * all, so destroying a superseded copy-fallback swapchain
+    * disconnected the swapchain that had taken the window from it.
+    *
+    * A swapchain that is no longer the owner has no framebuffer left to
+    * close: wsi_horizon_claim_window closed it at the moment it was
+    * evicted. So this is where every framebuffer is closed, exactly
+    * once, and always by the owner. */
    simple_mtx_lock(&chain->surface->lock);
-   if (chain->surface->owner == chain)
+   if (chain->surface->owner == chain) {
       wsi_horizon_release_slots(chain);
-   simple_mtx_unlock(&chain->surface->lock);
-
-   if (chain->fb_created) {
-      framebufferClose(&chain->fb);
-      chain->fb_created = false;
+      wsi_horizon_close_framebuffer(chain);
    }
+   simple_mtx_unlock(&chain->surface->lock);
 
    wsi_horizon_release_window(chain);
 
@@ -2146,8 +2194,10 @@ fail_images:
       result = VK_ERROR_INITIALIZATION_FAILED;
 
 fail_no_chain:
-   if (chain->fb_created)
-      framebufferClose(&chain->fb);
+   /* Still the owner on every path that reaches here: the window was
+    * claimed above and this swapchain has not been handed out, so
+    * nothing can have named it as an oldSwapchain and evicted it. */
+   wsi_horizon_close_framebuffer(chain);
    wsi_horizon_release_window(chain);
    if (chain->holds_nv_fence)
       nvFenceExit();
-- 
2.55.0.windows.2

